```php
function smart_sort_array($set, $order = 'ASC') {
    $size = count($set);
    for ($i = 0; $i < $size - 1; $i++) {
        for ($j = 0; $j < $size - $i - 1; $j++) {
            if (($order == 'DESC' && $set[$j]['age'] < $set[$j+1]['age']) || ($order == 'ASC' && $set[$j]['age'] > $set[$j+1]['age'])) {
                // Switch elements
                $temp = $set[$j];
                $set[$j] = $set[$j+1];
                $set[$j+1] = $temp;
            }
        }
    }
    return $set;
}
```

This function takes an associative array `$set` as its first argument, and an optional second argument `$order` that defaults to 'ASC' (ascending order). It uses a nested loop to compare adjacent elements in the array and switch them if they are not in the desired order based on their age values. Finally, it returns the sorted array.

