 Absolutely, you can store and manage binary data such as images in MongoDB using GridFS. GridFS is a specification for storing and retrieving files that exceed the BSON-document size limit of 16MB. It divides a file into chunks and stores each chunk as a separate document. This allows you to work with large files like images efficiently in MongoDB.

To use GridFS, you'll need to install a package like `gridfs-stream` or `mongoose-gridfs-storage` if you're using Node.js or Mongoose respectively. After installation, you can write image data to GridFS just like writing any other document:

```javascript
const fs = require('fs');
const GridFsStorage = require('multer-gridfs-storage');
const upload = multer({ storage: new GridFsStorage({ collection: 'images' }) });

app.post('/upload', upload.single('image'), function(req, res, next) {
  // 'image' is the name of the input field where the image is sent
  const image = req.file;
  res.send('Image uploaded successfully.');
});
```

In this example, an image is uploaded from an HTML form using Multer middleware, which automatically splits the file into chunks and stores it in the 'images' collection in MongoDB. You can later retrieve and manipulate the image data using GridFS APIs.

Keep in mind that storing binary data directly in databases may increase the overall database size and have some performance implications, so consider your specific use case and requirements before making a decision.

